<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Archbishopric of Magdeburg</h1><p class="st-meta">968 CE – 1680 CE · capital Magdeburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Otto the Great's favoured archbishopric on the Elbe, head of the church in the Slavic east, which became a Protestant territory and then part of Brandenburg-Prussia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Otto I founded a monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> in 937 and, after years of negotiation with the Pope and the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, raised it to an archbishopric in 968, with suffragans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeitz">Zeitz</a> (later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg">Naumburg</a>), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havelberg">Havelberg</a>. Otto and his first wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eadgyth">Edith of England</a>, are buried in the cathedral.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The archbishops ruled a territory along the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>. Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wichmann-von-seeburg">Wichmann</a> granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> its town law around 1188, a model copied across central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The Gothic cathedral, begun in 1209, was the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-brandenburg">Albert of Brandenburg's</a> sale of indulgences as archbishop prompted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Luther</a>'s theses in 1517.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> became Lutheran, ruled by Protestant administrators, and the city was sacked by imperial troops in 1631. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> assigned the archbishopric to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> on the death of its administrator, which happened in 1680, when it became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-magdeburg">Duchy of Magdeburg</a> within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/archbishopric-of-magdeburg.jpg" alt="Archbishopric of Magdeburg" width="320" height="265"><figcaption>Image: Gustav Droysen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hochstift_Halberstadt_1250.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>968 CE – 1680 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Magdeburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Adalbert of Magdeburg, Wichmann von Seeburg, Albert of Brandenburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Low German, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Prince-Archbishopric of Magdeburg, Erzstift Magdeburg</dd></dl></aside></div>
